diff --git a/packages/mobile/README.md b/packages/mobile/README.md
index 98adaf42e..b9f707e58 100644
--- a/packages/mobile/README.md
+++ b/packages/mobile/README.md
@@ -18,7 +18,7 @@ The mobile package reuses the web build, then rewrites `mobile.html` to `index.h
 - iOS 18 and later offer a "New OpenChamber Session" action in Shortcuts and Siri (`ios/App/App/OpenChamberAppShortcuts.swift`). It takes an optional project and an optional message: it opens the app on a new session draft in that project, or where the user last worked, and sends the message as the first one once the project's models and agents are loaded. The project picker lists the projects the app last wrote into the widget snapshot (`packages/ui/src/apps/mobileWidgetSnapshot.ts`); a project the connected server no longer has opens an unsent draft with the message in the composer. A push from another server resets the snapshot to that server, which leaves the picker empty until the app is opened again. A draft whose composer already holds text, files or attached context gets the message appended and sends nothing. The request reaches the page through the app-local `ShortcutRequests` plugin (`ShortcutRequests.swift`, `packages/ui/src/lib/nativeShortcutRequests.ts`), never through an `openchamber://` link, because any app or web page can open a link and a link must not send a prompt.
 - Android offers the entry point iOS has as a Control Center control, as a Quick Settings tile (`android/app/src/main/java/com/openchamber/app/NewSessionTileService.java`) that opens the app on a new session draft. It is a `TileService` that always reports inactive, because a tile is a toggle in the panel's model but there is nothing to toggle, and its tap opens the same `openchamber://new` deep link the iOS control opens. On a locked device the tap asks for unlock first. The tile's label and accessibility text are Android string resources in `res/values-*`, one per app locale, so they follow the system language; the `OpenChamber` subtitle stays untranslated. Its icon is the brand glyph generated by `scripts/brand/generate.mjs`.
 - Text, links and files can be shared to the app from the Android and iOS share sheets. `MainActivity` declares `ACTION_SEND`/`ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE` for any type, reads the intent off the UI thread into `SharedPayloadStore` (`SharedIntentReader`), and a share that arrives while the app runs comes through `onNewIntent`; a share counts as done only once the page has taken it, so a restored activity reads its intent again when the process died before the handoff, and never replays one the page already took. The page takes it through the app-local `SharedFiles` plugin (`android/app/src/main/java/com/openchamber/app/SharedFilesPlugin.java`), and `packages/ui/src/lib/nativeSharedFiles.ts` (Android with the plugin only) hands it to the deep-link navigation, which opens a new session draft of its own, leaving any draft the user was writing untouched, with the files attached unsent and local and a shared link or paragraph typed into the composer without being sent. Nothing is written to a server before Send: Office documents get the usual "Attach as is" / "Convert to text" choice, and "as is" keeps the original until the message is sent. The composer's own policy decides what can be attached, and every refusal is named in the "some files were skipped" toast, in the app's language: more than eight files, a file over 10 MB, a batch over 32 MB, an unsupported type or a HEIC the decoder cannot read, an empty file, a stream shorter than the size its provider promised, a URI that is not another app's `content://` URI, or a provider that stops responding (cut off after 15 s per file, 30 s per share so later shares are not stuck behind it). On iOS the `OpenChamberShare` extension has no screen: it copies the share, within the same limits and with the same refusal reasons, into the App Group inbox and posts a notification "Tap to open the draft in OpenChamber" after a one-second "Saved to OpenChamber" card (an alert with OK when notifications are off), because iOS lets a share extension open its app by no supported means. The app-local `SharedFiles` plugin (`ios/App/App/SharedFiles.swift`) hands every waiting share to the same page code whenever the app becomes active, oldest first. See `HANDOFF.md` for the guarantees and the tests behind them. The intent carries the content; the `openchamber://` link carries nothing by itself.
-- Long-pressing the app icon offers *New session* and *Dictate* on both platforms. Their titles are localized on both: iOS through the `SHORTCUT_NEW_SESSION_TITLE` and `SHORTCUT_DICTATE_TITLE` keys in `ios/App/App/*.lproj/InfoPlist.strings`, which `Info.plist` names, and Android through `shortcut_new_session` and `shortcut_dictate` in every `res/values-*`. *New session* opens the `openchamber://new` link. iOS keeps it as a static `UIApplicationShortcutItem` in `ios/App/App/Info.plist`, carrying that link, and `SceneDelegate` forwards the item to Capacitor as a URL (a cold launch arrives through `connectionOptions.shortcutItem`). Android keeps it in `android/app/src/main/res/xml/shortcuts.xml`, declared through `android.app.shortcuts` on the launcher activity — the shortcut service ignores an application-level copy — and it is an `ACTION_VIEW` intent on that link, which the manifest's `openchamber` filter is what lets through. On Android a shortcut that cold-starts the app comes back through `App.getLaunchUrl()`, because `appUrlOpen` only covers a running app. The link routes through the web layer's deep-link vocabulary (`packages/ui/src/apps/deepLinks.ts`). *Dictate* opens a new session draft whose composer starts recording as soon as it shows that draft (`packages/ui/src/components/chat/composer/DOCUMENTATION.md`). It carries no link, because any app or web page can open one and a link must not start the microphone: on iOS `SceneDelegate` puts it in the `ShortcutRequests` inbox by its item type, and on Android the shortcut targets the unexported `DictateShortcut` activity alias of `MainActivity` (`DictateShortcut.java`), which only the launcher, starting shortcuts as this app, can reach. Both hand it to the page through the app-local `ShortcutRequests` plugin (`packages/ui/src/lib/nativeShortcutRequests.ts`). A relaunch from Recents or a restored activity does not record again.
+- Long-pressing the app icon offers *New session*, *Waiting on me* and *Dictate* on both platforms. Their titles are localized on both: iOS through the `SHORTCUT_NEW_SESSION_TITLE`, `SHORTCUT_WAITING_TITLE` and `SHORTCUT_DICTATE_TITLE` keys in `ios/App/App/*.lproj/InfoPlist.strings`, which `Info.plist` names, and Android through `shortcut_new_session`, `shortcut_waiting` and `shortcut_dictate` in every `res/values-*`. *New session* opens the `openchamber://new` link, and *Waiting on me* opens `openchamber://sessions?filter=attention`, the session list narrowed to the sessions waiting on you. iOS keeps it as a static `UIApplicationShortcutItem` in `ios/App/App/Info.plist`, carrying that link, and `SceneDelegate` forwards the item to Capacitor as a URL (a cold launch arrives through `connectionOptions.shortcutItem`). Android keeps it in `android/app/src/main/res/xml/shortcuts.xml`, declared through `android.app.shortcuts` on the launcher activity — the shortcut service ignores an application-level copy — and it is an `ACTION_VIEW` intent on that link, which the manifest's `openchamber` filter is what lets through. On Android a shortcut that cold-starts the app comes back through `App.getLaunchUrl()`, because `appUrlOpen` only covers a running app. The link routes through the web layer's deep-link vocabulary (`packages/ui/src/apps/deepLinks.ts`). *Dictate* opens a new session draft whose composer starts recording as soon as it shows that draft (`packages/ui/src/components/chat/composer/DOCUMENTATION.md`). It carries no link, because any app or web page can open one and a link must not start the microphone: on iOS `SceneDelegate` puts it in the `ShortcutRequests` inbox by its item type, and on Android the shortcut targets the unexported `DictateShortcut` activity alias of `MainActivity` (`DictateShortcut.java`), which only the launcher, starting shortcuts as this app, can reach. Both hand it to the page through the app-local `ShortcutRequests` plugin (`packages/ui/src/lib/nativeShortcutRequests.ts`). A relaunch from Recents or a restored activity does not record again.
 - Downloads (file viewer, saved message images) open the native share sheet instead of a browser download, because a WebView has no download manager. iOS uses the Web Share API; Android WebView lacks it, so Android goes through the app-local `FileShare` plugin (`android/app/src/main/java/com/openchamber/app/FileSharePlugin.java`, registered in `MainActivity`), which writes the file to the cache directory and shares it through the manifest's `FileProvider`. The shared helper is `packages/ui/src/lib/nativeFileShare.ts`.
 - Image paste on Android goes through the input method, not the page. The IME reads the content types the focused editor declares in `EditorInfo.contentMimeTypes` and refuses an image paste when it finds none, which is why Gboard answers "image paste is not supported here" in a WebView and no paste event ever fires. `android/app/src/main/java/com/openchamber/app/OpenChamberWebView.java` declares image content and hands whatever the IME commits to the page as an `openchamber-native-image-paste` window event; `packages/ui/src/lib/nativeImagePaste.ts` turns that into a file and the composer attaches it the same way it attaches a pasted image. `MainActivity.load()` inflates that WebView in place of Capacitor's own, which is what makes the declaration take effect. The WebView is a single input connection for every editable element, so `ImagePastePlugin` narrows the declaration to the composer on focus and blur, and asks the IME to read it again when the composer takes focus, since the IME reads it at focus time and that can be before the report arrives. The media type comes from the bytes rather than from the clipboard description. Gboard is the only input method confirmed to insert content this way, and Android Chrome refuses the same paste, so a hosted mobile page still cannot do it.
 - The Changes workspace has a top-level Changes / Branch / Commit / Pull Requests selector. Checkout, Sync, staging, and commit controls appear only under Changes. Comparisons show a read-only file list that opens one diff at a time. Their shared source pickers use bottom sheets on phones and anchored popovers on tablets. Commit lists the latest 50 commits of the checked-out branch. Pull Requests shows the published GitHub diff, excluding local edits and unpushed commits, with search and an explicit refresh action. Closing the workspace preserves the current detail and suspends comparison reads; changing repository or instance resets navigation, and a new file link from chat opens the working-tree diff.
